      "raw_response": "Abstract:\nThis study generated knockin mouse models to investigate the role of SHP-2 and STAT3 pathways in immune responses. The results showed that the SHP2-deficient mice displayed symptoms of splenomegaly and lymphadenopathy, suggesting that disruption of the SHP2 pathway can lead to lymph node enlargement. In contrast, the STAT3-deficient mice died before birth, while the gp130-deficient mice exhibited a different phenotype. Moreover, SHP2-deficient mice had increased levels of Th1-type cytokine production and IgG2a and IgG2b, indicating that SHP2 has a negative regulatory role in immune responses. These findings imply that the balance of signals through gp130, including both STAT3- and SHP2-mediated pathways, is crucial for proper immune function.\n\nANSWER: SUPPORTED.",
